{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1MJeLxTJx7GAn1usqxr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. The theorem as stated is false, so it can't be proved.\n\n`IsTree T1` and `IsTree T2` are statements about graphs on the whole vertex type `V`, so each of `T1` and `T2` must be connected on all of `V`. The hypothesis `he : e ∈ T.edgeSet` forces `V` to have at least two distinct vertices. In a connected graph on such a `V`, every vertex has a neighbour, so its support is all of `V`.\n\nThat makes `T1.support` and `T2.support` both equal to `Set.univ`. Since `V` is nonempty, they cannot be disjoint. For example, take `T` to be the path on two vertices and `e` its one edge. The hypotheses hold, but no `T1` and `T2` satisfy the conclusion.\n\nI did not change `/work/combi_brualdi_ch11_59.lean`; it still contains the original `sorry`. The informal claim is true, but it needs trees on the two components' vertex sets rather than on all of `V`. That would mean changing the statement, which the rules don't allow."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1174,"cache_read_input_tokens":2206,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1174,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c5a3b6fe-2dc7-4b6d-a752-0b173bb4b542","uuid":"49441dbd-8a05-4bf9-a4bd-5f8efbb7343a","timestamp":"2026-10-14T02:36:13.766Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1MJe27NDxqCrE1Ri276"}
